intent: Navigation
	description=What the person asked the browser to do.
	url: URL
		description=The address the person typed or clicked.
		example=The address bar holds `example.com/pricing`.
	nav-request: Navigation request
		description=The browser's decision to fetch one top-level document.
		example=The browser commits to loading the pricing page.
policy: Standing rules
	description=Rules that already hold before the request leaves. They apply to every step, so they have no place in the order.
	hsts: HSTS list
		description=A list of sites the browser must reach over https.
		example=The browser rewrites `http://example.com` to `https://example.com` without asking the network.
	cookie-jar: Cookie jar
		description=Stored cookies the browser attaches to a matching request.
		example=The session cookie for `example.com` rides along with the request.
	cache-policy: Cache policy
		description=The response headers that say how long a copy stays fresh.
		example=`Cache-Control: max-age=600` lets the browser reuse the page for ten minutes.
store: Stored copies
	description=Copies the browser keeps so it can skip work.
	dns-cache: DNS cache
		description=Names the browser resolved recently.
		example=The browser already knows the address for `example.com` and skips the lookup.
	http-cache: HTTP cache
		description=Responses the browser already downloaded.
		example=A fresh copy of the pricing page is still in the cache.
naming: Name lookup
	description=Turning a name into an address.
	dns-query: DNS query
		description=A question sent to a resolver asking where a name lives.
		example=The browser asks the resolver for `example.com`.
	ip-address: IP address
		description=The address the browser will connect to.
		example=The resolver answers `93.184.216.34`.
connect: Connection
	description=Opening a channel the browser can trust.
	tcp-handshake: TCP handshake
		description=Three messages that open a reliable connection.
		example=SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK complete in one round trip.
	tls-handshake: TLS handshake
		description=The exchange that agrees on keys and checks the certificate.
		example=The browser verifies the certificate for `example.com`.
	secure-channel: Secure channel
		description=An open connection the browser can send private bytes over.
		example=The connection is ready to carry the request.
transfer: Transfer
	description=Asking for bytes and receiving them.
	http-request: HTTP request
		description=The message that asks the server for one resource.
		example=`GET /pricing` goes out with the session cookie.
	http-response: HTTP response
		description=The server's reply: a status, headers, and a body.
		example=`200 OK` arrives with the page body.
	html-bytes: HTML bytes
		description=The raw document body, arriving in pieces.
		example=The first chunk of markup reaches the parser before the last chunk leaves the server.
	subresource-request: Subresource request
		description=A request for a file the page refers to.
		example=The browser asks for the stylesheet named in the head.
	css-bytes: CSS bytes
		description=The raw stylesheet body.
		example=`main.css` arrives.
	js-bytes: JavaScript bytes
		description=The raw script body.
		example=`app.js` arrives.
parse: Parsing
	description=Turning bytes into trees the renderer can use.
	token-stream: Token stream
		description=The markup broken into tags, attributes, and text.
		example=The parser emits a `<head>` start tag, then a `<link>` tag.
	preload-scanner: Preload scanner
		description=A second reader that races ahead of the parser to find files worth fetching early. It runs beside the main path, not inside it.
		example=While the parser is still in the head, the scanner already asked for the hero image.
	dom-tree: DOM tree
		description=The document as a tree of nodes.
		example=The body, its sections, and its text become linked nodes.
	cssom-tree: CSSOM tree
		description=The stylesheet as a tree of rules the browser can match.
		example=Selectors and their declarations become a queryable tree.
	script-execution: Script execution
		description=Running a script that can read and change the document.
		example=`app.js` runs and inserts a banner into the body.
render: Rendering
	description=Turning trees into pixels.
	render-tree: Render tree
		description=Only the nodes that will be drawn, each with its computed style.
		example=A `display: none` menu is left out of the tree.
	layout-boxes: Layout boxes
		description=The size and position of every drawn node.
		example=The hero heading gets a box 1180 pixels wide at the top of the page.
	paint-records: Paint records
		description=The ordered drawing instructions for those boxes.
		example=Fill this rectangle, then draw this text on top.
	composited-frame: Composited frame
		description=The painted layers combined into one image.
		example=The fixed header layer sits over the scrolling content layer.
	first-paint: First contentful paint
		description=The moment the person first sees real content.
		example=The heading appears on screen.
